/**
 * Blue is the colour that arrives last. Berlin and Kay's staged sequence puts black, white and red first — which is exactly the set this atlas already carried — and blue only after green and yellow, so a great many languages name the sky with a word that also names grass. **110 of the 357 rows here do**, against 201 that keep the two apart, and the division is regional rather than scattered: Turkic kök runs from Kazakh and Kyrgyz through Tatar, Bashkir, Nogai, Kumyk and Sakha and meets Mongolic хөх; Celtic glas covers Welsh, Breton, Cornish and Old Irish; Tupí-Guaraní has hovy; Nguni -luhlaza and Sotho-Tswana tala carry southern Africa. Kalmyk and Ossetian give the identical string for blue and green in two independent datasets, as do Sui, Hlai, Rotuman, Nusu, Juǀ'hoan, Taa and Warao. Where a language does have a separate blue, it is often borrowed: 8 of the 20 Bantu rows here take buluu, bururu, bule or bululu from English, French or Swahili and lay it on top of an inherited grue term, and Bambara bulaman is French bleu — the inherited Mande system had no blue at all. A third pattern points the other way: Yoruba dúdú and Akan tuntum are the same word this map gives for BLACK, and Christaller wrote in 1881 that tuntum covers dark blue and dark green alike. Japanese 青 and Korean 파랗다 are the famous near-miss — both still cover green in fixed phrases, 青信号 and 파란 신호등 — but 緑 and 초록 are basic terms now and both languages are coded as separating the two, so they sit on the distinct side with the story kept in the note.
 */
